<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Paul IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1476">1476</a>–<a href="/years/1559">1559</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Gian Pietro Carafa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul IV, born Gian Pietro Carafa, was pope from 1555 to 1559 and a leading figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>. A co-founder of the Theatine order, he strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-inquisition">Roman Inquisition</a> and issued the first papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/index-librorum-prohibitorum">Index of Forbidden Books</a> in 1559. His bull Cum nimis absurdum of 1555 confined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to a walled ghetto, and his war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ended in defeat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iv.jpg" alt="Pope Paul IV" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manner of Jacopino del Conte · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pope_Paul_IV_%E2%80%93_Jacopino_Conte_(Manner),_ca._1560.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gian Pietro Carafa was born at Capriglia Irpina, near Avellino, into the prominent Carafa family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. His relative Cardinal Oliviero Carafa resigned the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chieti">Chieti</a> in his favour. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a> he was ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and then nuncio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where he conceived a violent detestation of Spanish rule that affected his later policies. In 1524 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Pope Clement VII</a> allowed him to resign his benefices and join the Theatines. He was made a cardinal in December 1536 and Archbishop of Naples. After the Regensburg Colloquy of 1541 failed, he persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a> to set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-inquisition">Roman Inquisition</a>, with himself as one of its Inquisitors-General.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Carafa was a surprise choice to succeed Pope Marcellus II. He was elected on 23 May 1555 and crowned on 26 May, accepting apparently because Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> opposed his election. Austere and authoritarian, he used the Holy Office against the Spirituali and imprisoned Cardinal Giovanni Morone, whom he suspected of being a hidden Protestant.</p><p>His bull Cum nimis absurdum of 17 July 1555 aimed to lead the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to convert. It restricted their trades, forbade them Christian servants, required yellow hats, and allowed one synagogue per city, so that seven were destroyed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It also ordered a walled ghetto in Rome, near the Rione Sant'Angelo, with a single gate locked at sundown; the Jews paid the costs. In 1556 about a hundred Marranos of Ancona were imprisoned and 25 Conversos who had reverted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> were burned. The Roman ghetto lasted until the end of the Papal States in 1870.</p><p>Displeased by the five-year truce between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> of February 1556, Paul urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a> to invade Spanish Naples. On 1 September 1556 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> invaded the Papal States with 12,000 men under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a>. After French forces were defeated at Civitella del Tronto in August 1557, Paul agreed to neutrality in the Peace of Cave-Palestrina of 12 September 1557. After scandals he banished his nephews from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1559.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Paul IV died on 18 August 1559. Crowds rioted even before his death. They decapitated his statue on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Campidoglio</a> and threw it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>, freed over 400 prisoners from the city jails, killed the Inquisitor Tommaso Scotti, burned the Inquisition's offices and removed the Carafa arms from buildings across the city. His nephew had him buried without ceremony, and in 1566 Pius V moved his remains to Santa Maria sopra <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Paul IV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jews of Rome, present since antiquity, were confined to a ghetto by <b>Paul IV</b> in 1555 and remained subject to restrictions until 1870.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Capriglia Irpina, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.96,14.78&amp;year=1529" aria-label="Capriglia Irpina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pope Paul IV"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5944 4007h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5944 4007h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.96,14.78&amp;year=1529">What was here in 1529? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
